  "revision": 47,
  "assignment_text": "Prepare a five-slide presentation for a master's seminar on digital systems in public services. Your audience is course examiners and classmates who have not worked on waste sorting technology. Use the fictional North Campus sorting pilot in the attached source PDF as a case study. The presentation should take no more than four minutes.\n\nExplain the sorting problem, the proposed camera-assisted workflow, the pilot observations, the operating trade-offs, and a measured conclusion. Include at least one page-numbered reference to the source PDF when discussing the pilot observations. Distinguish observations from causal claims: the synthetic pilot has no randomized control group and cannot prove that the system caused the change. Do not present the figures as real research or a real campus result.\n\nKeep the five-slide order easy to follow and use concise English. The presentation must preserve the exact qualification “promising, not proven” in the conclusion. Do not add external statistics, invented citations, or recommendations about deploying the system beyond this fictional campus. The final PPTX should remain editable so the student can correct wording before presenting.",
  "context_pack_text": "Topic: Fictional North Campus waste sorting pilot\nPurpose: Explain a fictional camera-assisted sorting pilot to master's students and course examiners.\nAudience: Master's students and course examiners.\nProblem: Sorting errors make it harder to keep reusable materials separate on a campus.\nThesis: The fictional pilot is promising, not proven: fewer items were sorted incorrectly, but the study design cannot establish causation.\nClaim 1: In this synthetic four-week case, the observed incorrect-sorting share changed from 42% to 29% across 1,000 inspected items per week. See the demo source, page 1.\nClaim 2: The workflow flags uncertain items for staff review instead of deciding every item automatically. See the demo source, page 2.\nTrade-offs: Staff review takes 18 minutes per day and camera maintenance takes two hours per week in this synthetic case. See the demo source, page 2.\nConclusion: The results are promising, not proven; test the workflow in a controlled study before making a deployment decision.\nConstraints: Five slides, four minutes, concise English, preserve “promising, not proven”, cite PDF pages, and identify all numbers as synthetic.\nUnresolved question: Would the observed change persist on another campus without adding too much staff work?\nVerified material: Only statements directly recorded in the attached synthetic PDF are source material for this software demo.\nAssumptions: Transfer to other campuses and a causal impact are unverified.",
